### A.6 Adversarial Misalignment Rate Bound

**Claim.** For a system with true worst‑case misalignment rate \(M^*\), if we estimate \(\hat M_n = \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}_n} \bar Y_p\) from \(n\) independent probes, then for any \(\epsilon > 0\),

\[
\mathbb{P}\left( \hat M_n \le M^* - \epsilon \right) \le \exp(-2n\epsilon^2),
\]
\[
\mathbb{P}\left( \hat M_n \ge M^* + \epsilon \right) \le n \exp(-2n\epsilon^2).
\]

*Proof.* By Hoeffding’s inequality, for each probe \(p\), \(\mathbb{P}(|\bar Y_p - \mathbb{E}[Y_p]| \ge \epsilon) \le 2\exp(-2n_p\epsilon^2)\). Assuming each probe is evaluated \(n\) times, the max over \(n\) probes concentrates more slowly. Using union bound:

\[
\mathbb{P}\left( \hat M_n \le M^* - \epsilon \right) = \mathbb{P}\left( \forall p, \bar Y_p \le M^* - \epsilon \right) \le \mathbb{P}\left( \bar Y_{p^*} \le M^* - \epsilon \right) \le \exp(-2n\epsilon^2),
\]
where \(p^*\) is the probe achieving the worst‑case expected value. For the other side,
\[
\mathbb{P}\left( \hat M_n \ge M^* + \epsilon \right) \le \sum_{p} \mathbb{P}\left( \bar Y_p \ge \mathbb{E}[Y_p] + \epsilon \right) \le n \exp(-2n\epsilon^2).
\]
These bounds ensure that with enough probes and enough trials per probe, the empirical max is close to the true worst‑case rate. ∎

